---
title: Command shortcuts while editing
description: Let a Save command work in a focused editor without stealing text editing keys.
---

<span hidden data-pagefind-filter="SDK:preview.3 candidate"></span>

**Unreleased preview.3 candidate.** This option is not in the published preview.2
SDK. The released [preview.2 Commands reference](/LUI/docs/sdk-0.1.0-preview.2/libraries/luiworkbench/commands/)
keeps its original behavior. No new SDK download is published by this page.

Commands usually ignores keyboard shortcuts while a text box is focused. That
protects typing, but also prevents an app's Ctrl+S action from running in an
editor. In the candidate library, set `AllowWhileEditing = true` on that command.

Create a workbench project with the prepared preview.3 candidate SDK. Keep its
vendored libraries, manifest and module lock together. Replace `Src/Main.luau`
with this complete example, then use the candidate SDK's `lui check` and `lui run`:

```luau
{{Example}}
```

Focus the editor and press Ctrl+S. The status reports the captured byte count
and your text stays in the editor. This example stores the snapshot in memory;
it does not write a file. An application can bind the same command to a button
and use its existing storage service inside the callback.

The default is `false`. Opt-in needs a Control shortcut without Alt. Plain
typing and AltGr cannot trigger it; Shift must match the registered binding
exactly. Repeated key notifications are ignored. Native composition suppresses
`Window.Shortcut` before Commands runs.

Text editing still has precedence when an undo stack is empty. These Control
keys, with or without Shift, cannot opt in: A/C/V/X/Y/Z, Insert/Delete/Backspace,
Home/End, arrow keys, PageUp/PageDown, Enter/Return and Tab. Register such a command
without the flag if it should work outside an editor. Invalid registration throws
a bounded `[LUI:Commands]` error; validate configuration or catch expected errors
with `pcall`. A command callback failure returns `false, 'Failed'` from Execute
or Dispatch without exposing the callback's error text.

The flag is copied when the command is registered. Changing your original table
later does not change the binding. `List()` reports the effective boolean.
Dispose the registry when its owner is destroyed; it does not cancel tasks or
storage requests started by application callbacks.

The library is portable Luau over the existing scheduler and Window shortcut
contract. Focus, modifiers, composition and retirement have Windows/Linux
headless owner-input tests. Physical keyboard, IME and native host behavior are
qualified separately; a host without shortcut notifications emits no event.

